# JavaScript — Senior Shopify Theme Developer Rules

## General Standards
- Use vanilla JS only — Dawn ships with no jQuery; do not add it
- Use ES6+ syntax: `const`/`let`, arrow functions, template literals, destructuring
- Use `class` syntax matching Dawn's patterns (e.g., `class CustomComponent extends HTMLElement {}`)
- Register custom elements with `customElements.define('custom-[name]', ClassName)`
- Always use `customElements.whenDefined('component-name')` before interacting with Dawn's components

## Custom Elements Pattern (Follow Dawn's Architecture)
```js
class CustomHeroBanner extends HTMLElement {
  connectedCallback() {
    this.init();
  }
  init() {
    // setup logic
  }
}
customElements.define('custom-hero-banner', CustomHeroBanner);
```

## Event Handling
- Use event delegation over direct listeners where possible
- Always clean up listeners in `disconnectedCallback` for custom elements
- Use `document.addEventListener('shopify:section:load', handler)` to re-init after theme editor reloads
- Support theme editor events: `shopify:section:load`, `shopify:section:unload`, `shopify:block:select`, `shopify:block:deselect`

## Cart & AJAX
- Use the Shopify AJAX Cart API: `POST /cart/add.js`, `POST /cart/change.js`, `GET /cart.js`
- Always handle `fetch` errors — show user-friendly messages
- Use `routes.cart_add_url` (available in Dawn's global JS) instead of hardcoded `/cart/add`
- Dispatch `cart:refresh` or use Dawn's `PubSub` for cart count updates

## Performance
- Defer non-critical JS: use `<script defer>` or dynamic `import()`
- Use `IntersectionObserver` for scroll-triggered animations/lazy loading
- Avoid long tasks — break up work with `requestAnimationFrame` or `setTimeout`
- Do not block the main thread with synchronous fetch/XHR

## Dawn Integration
- Use Dawn's `PubSub` utility for event publishing: `publish(PUB_SUB_EVENTS.cartUpdate, data)`
- Use Dawn's `onKeyUpEscape` utility for modal/drawer close on Escape
- Extend, don't replace, Dawn's custom elements when possible

## Code Quality
- No `console.log` in production code — use a debug flag pattern
- Always handle null checks: `document.querySelector()` can return null
- Use `?.` optional chaining and `??` nullish coalescing
- Keep functions small and single-purpose
